1
00:00:00,780 --> 00:00:01,490
Hi.

2
00:00:01,770 --> 00:00:08,460
In this lesson, we are talking about meetings. Because meetings are such a big part of our day to day

3
00:00:08,460 --> 00:00:12,540
routine as professionals in the information technology industry.

4
00:00:12,630 --> 00:00:19,470
We either lead a meeting, we run a meeting, organize it, or we just participate in a meeting.

5
00:00:19,470 --> 00:00:27,510
But either way, we need to be able to express ourselves during the meetings to keep the discussion

6
00:00:27,510 --> 00:00:31,530
going, and we need to be confident in doing that.

7
00:00:31,530 --> 00:00:38,130
So this lesson is going to be useful for everybody, but maybe just a little bit more useful for people

8
00:00:38,130 --> 00:00:46,830
like project managers and team leaders and anyone who needs to conduct a meeting from time to time and

9
00:00:46,830 --> 00:00:52,140
organize the discussion and organize people within the meeting.

10
00:00:52,170 --> 00:00:55,200
So this lesson is going to have two parts.

11
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:59,730
In the first part, we are going to look at vocabulary.

12
00:01:00,420 --> 00:01:09,480
Lots of the words really focused on meetings, about the meetings, what we can talk about during the

13
00:01:09,480 --> 00:01:10,080
meeting.

14
00:01:10,410 --> 00:01:14,610
And then you're going to have a short break to do a quiz.

15
00:01:14,610 --> 00:01:23,340
And in the second part of the lesson, we are going to look at phrases and sentences, how to formulate

16
00:01:23,340 --> 00:01:31,740
everything from the beginning of the meeting, welcoming participants and all the way through the meeting

17
00:01:31,740 --> 00:01:39,300
with organizing the discussion, moving on from one point to another point in the discussion and all

18
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:44,550
the way to the meeting closing, how to close and say goodbye.

19
00:01:44,550 --> 00:01:49,860
So all of these things are going to be in the second part.

20
00:01:49,860 --> 00:01:51,690
So let's not waste time.

21
00:01:51,750 --> 00:01:53,940
Let's dive in into the first part.

22
00:01:56,060 --> 00:01:58,430
The first part is useful words.

23
00:01:58,610 --> 00:02:00,230
So let's look at that.

24
00:02:00,260 --> 00:02:02,660
The first word is agenda.

25
00:02:02,720 --> 00:02:03,400
Agenda.

26
00:02:03,410 --> 00:02:07,040
This is probably the most important word for any meeting.

27
00:02:07,070 --> 00:02:09,470
Every meeting has an agenda.

28
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:17,840
Agenda is a list of objectives, list of goals or topics to discuss in a meeting or during a meeting.

29
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:25,610
Example. When you create an online meeting, please always put the agenda in the invitation as well as.

30
00:02:25,610 --> 00:02:26,540
That's good practice.

31
00:02:27,980 --> 00:02:32,750
Apologies - announcing that some people are absent.

32
00:02:33,380 --> 00:02:40,430
Usually those people ask beforehand to give their apologies at the meeting of the they cannot come.

33
00:02:40,430 --> 00:02:43,400
So we say apologies for absence.

34
00:02:43,790 --> 00:02:44,510
Example.

35
00:02:45,230 --> 00:02:46,070
Hi, Jane.

36
00:02:46,460 --> 00:02:52,230
I would wouldn't be able to join the weekly team meeting today as I have a client meeting.

37
00:02:52,250 --> 00:02:53,990
Please give my apologies.

38
00:02:57,200 --> 00:02:58,340
Brainstorm.

39
00:02:58,550 --> 00:03:04,640
Brainstorm is the activity when everyone is a team is thinking and coming up with ideas.

40
00:03:04,640 --> 00:03:11,900
So everyone in the team is trying to give some idea on a particular topic. That's brainstorming and then

41
00:03:11,900 --> 00:03:18,560
we gather all of those ideas and we talk them through and we see which one is the best way.

42
00:03:19,180 --> 00:03:26,320
Example. Let's take a few minutes and brainstorm how we can improve cooperation between departments.

43
00:03:28,480 --> 00:03:31,390
The next word is chairperson or chair.

44
00:03:32,860 --> 00:03:35,440
Is a person who leads a meeting.

45
00:03:36,470 --> 00:03:38,200
As simple as that. Example.

46
00:03:38,890 --> 00:03:45,700
As Chair, I want to take a moment to thank everybody for participating and sharing your thoughts and

47
00:03:45,700 --> 00:03:46,240
ideas.

48
00:03:47,100 --> 00:03:51,660
Now this word chairperson or chair is kind of a more formal.

49
00:03:51,660 --> 00:04:00,300
So we talk about a chairperson really with with more formal level of meetings. When you have just a regular

50
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:06,960
team meeting to talk with your team about the ongoing processes, you don't call yourself a chair,

51
00:04:07,560 --> 00:04:11,820
but in the more formal ones that will be a chairperson.

52
00:04:13,620 --> 00:04:14,460
Minutes.

53
00:04:14,820 --> 00:04:22,079
Minutes is a written record or a written document of what was said at the meeting.

54
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:26,420
Can be detailed, but usually is just in the form of bullet points.

55
00:04:26,440 --> 00:04:32,610
So this and this was set this and this was decided upon. Example.

56
00:04:32,820 --> 00:04:39,990
First of all, let's quickly review the meeting minutes from last week and see if we have any open issues.

57
00:04:40,620 --> 00:04:47,100
So minutes or meeting minutes, you can call them either way.

58
00:04:48,960 --> 00:04:55,410
Designate. To designate means to assign, to ask someone to do something.

59
00:04:55,680 --> 00:05:01,140
Example. Does anybody volunteer to take the minutes or shall I designate someone?

60
00:05:04,550 --> 00:05:05,910
Formality.

61
00:05:05,930 --> 00:05:07,200
Formality.

62
00:05:07,220 --> 00:05:13,290
Formality is a procedure that has to be followed due to a rule or due to a law,

63
00:05:13,310 --> 00:05:21,320
for example. I will schedule a weekly meeting and take care of all the formalities so that the team

64
00:05:21,320 --> 00:05:23,180
can concentrate on their work.

65
00:05:23,300 --> 00:05:31,310
So we can call scheduling meetings, organizing different documents, records and so on.

66
00:05:31,310 --> 00:05:35,630
We can call those things formalities because they are important,

67
00:05:35,630 --> 00:05:36,650
we should follow them,

68
00:05:36,650 --> 00:05:43,790
we have to follow them, because it's the rule or it's the policy of our company or whatever.

69
00:05:43,790 --> 00:05:46,520
But this is not something that we create.

70
00:05:46,820 --> 00:05:52,430
This is not work done for the actual product, for example.

71
00:05:53,120 --> 00:05:59,810
But these are all of the organisational issues that we still have to have to do, so and we sometimes

72
00:05:59,810 --> 00:06:01,280
call them formalities.

73
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:06,280
The next word is objectives.

74
00:06:06,580 --> 00:06:09,190
Objectives are goals.

75
00:06:09,520 --> 00:06:11,380
Goals to accomplish.

76
00:06:11,590 --> 00:06:16,960
Or we can also say that we have objectives for the meeting.

77
00:06:16,960 --> 00:06:24,580
It means that those are the topics to discuss at the meeting, and usually those objectives are in the

78
00:06:24,580 --> 00:06:25,330
agenda.

79
00:06:26,140 --> 00:06:32,230
Example. I'm happy that we covered all the objectives today within the designated time.

80
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:44,750
Participant, participant or attendee, attendee is a person who comes to a meeting or joins in the online

81
00:06:44,750 --> 00:06:45,440
meeting.

82
00:06:46,520 --> 00:06:48,560
You know the word to participate.

83
00:06:48,560 --> 00:06:56,900
So a person who participates is a participant. The same way a person who attends the meeting is an attendee.

84
00:06:57,950 --> 00:07:02,810
Example. How many participants were there at the last year's conference?

85
00:07:03,790 --> 00:07:05,590
How many attendees?

86
00:07:07,310 --> 00:07:08,240
The next work.

87
00:07:08,240 --> 00:07:09,790
Show of hands.

88
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:16,370
Show of hands - raised hands to express an opinion in a vote.

89
00:07:16,820 --> 00:07:20,600
And you can have two options, usually for or against.

90
00:07:21,350 --> 00:07:28,310
Example, let's decide if we need a short break with the show of hands, please raise your hand.

91
00:07:28,490 --> 00:07:29,870
Who is for it?

92
00:07:31,110 --> 00:07:31,510
Okay.

93
00:07:31,620 --> 00:07:34,410
So who is for break?

94
00:07:34,410 --> 00:07:39,020
And then people just raise their hands and they get counted.

95
00:07:39,030 --> 00:07:44,480
So we vote this way. To wrap up.

96
00:07:44,490 --> 00:07:47,130
To wrap up means to finish.

97
00:07:47,130 --> 00:07:48,420
Simply to finish.

98
00:07:49,180 --> 00:07:49,750
Okay.

99
00:07:49,750 --> 00:07:53,170
Let's wrap up for today and I'll see you all next week.

100
00:07:56,010 --> 00:08:01,170
And now a couple of words about online meetings and all of the specifics.

101
00:08:01,170 --> 00:08:08,550
So when you create an invitation for an online meeting, for example, in Outlook, MS Teams, Zoom or whatever

102
00:08:08,550 --> 00:08:15,950
else you're using, you usually need to indicate required and optional participants.

103
00:08:15,960 --> 00:08:17,820
What does it mean required?

104
00:08:18,210 --> 00:08:23,330
It means that it is necessary for that person to be present at the meeting.

105
00:08:23,340 --> 00:08:24,480
They are required.

106
00:08:24,480 --> 00:08:25,410
They need to be.

107
00:08:25,410 --> 00:08:32,730
there. Optional means they can decide if they want to join or not, or if they need to join this meeting

108
00:08:32,730 --> 00:08:33,419
or not.

109
00:08:35,460 --> 00:08:40,140
When a participant gets the invitation, they respond to it.

110
00:08:40,799 --> 00:08:48,030
It's called to respond to an invitation, and they usually have three types of responses.

111
00:08:49,020 --> 00:08:53,280
It's to accept, tentatively accept or decline.

112
00:08:53,790 --> 00:08:59,790
Well, accept means "yes, I will attend". Tentatively accept means

113
00:08:59,790 --> 00:09:04,620
"I don't know yet" and decline means "no, I will not attend".

114
00:09:05,160 --> 00:09:12,960
So I think it's clear with accept and decline and this tentatively accept means that you don't want

115
00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:20,070
to decline right away, but you are not sure if you can come or if you need to come to that meeting.

116
00:09:20,070 --> 00:09:22,770
So you click tentative.

117
00:09:23,970 --> 00:09:27,030
Again, this word is here with the transcription.

118
00:09:27,060 --> 00:09:28,080
Tentative.

119
00:09:28,110 --> 00:09:30,360
It means that you are still thinking.

120
00:09:33,310 --> 00:09:40,260
When a participant receives an invitation, they can also forward the invitation to a colleague.

121
00:09:40,330 --> 00:09:42,610
If you want to invite somebody else

122
00:09:42,610 --> 00:09:47,950
you forward the invitation just like you forward an email or whatever.

123
00:09:48,040 --> 00:09:52,390
So to forward means to send the original invitation to someone else.

124
00:09:54,150 --> 00:09:58,410
In an online meeting, there sometimes can be a so called lobby.

125
00:09:58,440 --> 00:10:05,700
It means that when a person, for example, that can be somebody outside of your organization, if they

126
00:10:05,700 --> 00:10:09,430
join a meeting, they first get into the lobby.

127
00:10:09,450 --> 00:10:19,140
It means that they wait until somebody lets them in the actual meeting room, and then the organizer

128
00:10:19,140 --> 00:10:22,360
of the meeting needs to admit them.

129
00:10:22,380 --> 00:10:28,740
Usually you will have this pop up window that's going to say Participants are waiting in the lobby and

130
00:10:28,740 --> 00:10:36,240
you just click admit for all of them and they get they are let into the actual meeting room so that

131
00:10:36,240 --> 00:10:37,580
you can communicate.

132
00:10:37,590 --> 00:10:43,440
So it's called a lobby and it's called to admit those participants.

133
00:10:43,860 --> 00:10:51,340
Now, more of a similar concepts and similar words are also in our lecture

134
00:10:51,360 --> 00:10:56,070
Working remotely with Lilly so you can check it out if you still haven't.

135
00:10:56,760 --> 00:10:59,910
And we will stop at this point.

136
00:11:00,180 --> 00:11:01,500
Please do the quiz.

137
00:11:01,500 --> 00:11:10,230
And the next part of the lesson is going to be centered about around the meeting, around how the meeting

138
00:11:10,230 --> 00:11:17,430
is started, how it goes through, and how we close the meeting with more useful phrases and sentences.

